## Reviewer
### Summary

This paper proposes a method for creating synthetic instruction tuning data. The proposed method consists of two steps: (1) extracting skills from a powerful LLM (GPT-4 in this paper), and (2) generating questions and answers based on the extracted skills. The authors show that the proposed method can be used to improve the performance of instruction-following models.

### Soundness

3 good
### Presentation

3 good
### Contribution

2 fair
### Strengths

- The proposed method is simple and effective. The method is easy to follow and reproduce.

- The method is not limited to instruction-following tasks, and can be applied to other tasks.

- The authors provide a lot of ablation studies to show the effectiveness of the proposed method.

### Weaknesses

- The authors only evaluated the proposed method on instruction-following tasks. It would be better to evaluate the proposed method on other tasks, such as question-answering, summarization, etc.

- The authors only compared the proposed method with SFT on Alpaca-52K. It would be better to compare the proposed method with other methods, such as Self-Instruct, Alpaca-52K, and UltraChat.

- The authors only evaluated the proposed method on AlpacaEval 2.0. It would be better to evaluate the proposed method on other benchmarks, such as WildBench and MT-Bench.

- The authors only used GPT-4 as the teacher model. It would be better to evaluate the proposed method with other teacher models, such as LLaMA-2 and Vicuna.

- The authors did not evaluate the generalization ability of the proposed method. It would be better to evaluate the proposed method on out-of-domain tasks.

- The authors did not evaluate the robustness of the proposed method. It would be better to evaluate the proposed method on adversarial attacks.

