### Summary

This paper proposes a novel method for backmapping coarse-grained (CG) protein structures to all-atom (AA) structures. The method is based on a denoising diffusion model that operates in a latent space. The latent space is learned by a VQ-VAE that encodes the protein structure into a discrete latent space. The diffusion model is conditioned on the CG structure. The method is evaluated on three protein datasets, and it outperforms the baselines in terms of structural accuracy and chemical validity.

### Soundness

3 good

### Presentation

3 good

### Contribution

3 good

### Strengths

- The paper is well-written and easy to follow.
- The method is novel and interesting. The use of a VQ-VAE to learn a discrete latent space for the protein structure is a good idea. The diffusion model is conditioned on the CG structure, which is a good idea as it can help to preserve the structural information.
- The method is evaluated on three protein datasets, and it outperforms the baselines in terms of structural accuracy and chemical validity.
- The ablation study is informative and shows the importance of the different components of the method.

### Weaknesses

- The method is not evaluated on the ATLAS dataset in the main text. Only the best and worst cases are shown in the supplementary material. I think it would be better to show the results on the whole dataset in the main text.
- The method is not compared with other baselines such as CG2AA [1] and CG2AT [2].
- The method is not compared with other diffusion-based methods such as DiAMoNDBack [3] and Towards Protein Backmapping with Diffusion Models [4].
- The method is not evaluated on other metrics such as RMSD and GED.
- The method is not evaluated on other protein datasets such as PDBbind [5] and PDBRedo [6].

